// NEGATIVE TEST -- a macro body may not contain a two-character operator (N3-12).
//
// `AstToken::Punct` holds ONE `char`, so `==` has no representation in a stored
// macro body. It is not a matter of listing it: storing it as two puncts would
// change the program, because `= =` is not `==`.
//
// WHAT IT USED TO DO: the parser's token table ended in
// `_ => AstToken::Ident(format!("{:?}", token))`, so `==` became an IDENTIFIER
// NAMED `EqEq` and the failure surfaced three phases later as
// "Undefined variable or function: 'EqEq'" -- a name the author never wrote,
// pointing at no line they can fix. Measured at e8eb1a9 on this exact body, and
// on `assert!(1 == 1)`, which fails the same way for the same reason and is why
// the `assert!` builtin cannot be used with the comparison it exists for.
//
// The same refusal covers `!=`, `<=`, `>=`, `&&`, `||`, `->`, `::`, `=>` and
// `..`. Single-character operators are fine: `<`, `>`, `+`, `%`, `&` and the
// rest round-trip, and tests/03_macros.pd uses several.
macro is_five!(n) { $n == 5 }

fn main() {
    if is_five!(5) {
        print("five");
    }
}
